<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:173%;">Appendix </span></p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>More Discussion on Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Large language models</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs are often Transformer-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> equipped with tmassive parameter sizes and extensive pretraining data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. The training pipeline of LLM often involce pretraining and post-training. LLMs commonly adopt auto-regressive pretraining strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Significant research has focused on post-training methods to adapt LLMs for various tasks, such as multitask finetuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, instruction tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, in-context learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>, and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>. As LLMs continue to scale in size, numerous studies have focused on improving their deployment efficiency, including memory management <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and inference acceleration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>In-context learning and Chain-of-Thought</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In-context learning.</span>
LLM exhibits a remarkable ability for in-context learning (ICL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which allows pretrained LLMs to solve specific tasks by conditioning on a few prepended in-context examples, without requiring any updates to the model parameters.
Several empirical studies investigate the behavior of ICLs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> formulate the problems and analyze the sensitivity of LLMs to in-context examples sequences. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> investigate on how LLMs performance change react to the ground-truth label and text demonstrations within in-context examples.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> propose methods to effective selection of in-context learning examples.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> use meta training with an explicit in-context learning object to enhance performance.
Theoretically, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> provide a bayesian framework to explain the working mechanism of in-context learning.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> investigate with linear models, showing how transformers can represent gradient descent and conduct linear regression. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> provide analysis on how ICL works in non-linear functions. Based on these works, we present an analysis demonstrating how LLMs can exhibit compositional capabilities in ICL tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-Thought reasoning.</span>
Chain of thought (CoT) is widely used to solve multi-step reasoning questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>. CoT generates
an intermediate reasoning process in language before outputting the final answer. Typical CoT methods prompt LLMs to produce these intermediate steps either in zero-shot or few-shot settings. Zero-shot-CoT adds instructions such as "Let’s think step by step" in prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> while few-shot-CoT provides several examples with step-by-step reasoning as in in-context demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>. Typical few-shot-CoT improves LLM’s reasoning ability with manually designed demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>. Another line of research focuses on automatically selecting demonstrations, eliminating the need for manual construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite>.
Several theoretical work have been proposed to analyze the effecitiveness of CoT. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> studies the expressiveness of shallow
transformers, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> further shows CoT allows for performing more serial computations that a vanilla transformer without CoT, increasing the effective depth of a transformer. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> present a uniform framework that allows for universal representability and computationally tractable chain-of-thought learning. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> analyzes the task generalization enabled by composition. Our theoretical analysis is partially inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> but considers a different setting with both simple/composite task examples, and also analyzes when the composition can fail.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Compositional task learning</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Solving complex tasks and reasoning of LLM is an active area of LLM research field <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. There is a line of empirical works explored the compositional capabilities of LLMs in abstract reasoning tasks under ICL settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> show LLMs are capable of learning abstract reasoning (e.g., grammar) to perform new tasks when finetuned or appropriate in-context examples. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> show that LLMs can handle simple sub-tasks, but often struggle with tasks that require composing multiple sub-tasks. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> shows that such challenges can be mitigated through the use of chain-of-thought prompting. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> reveals LLMs trained on relations like “A is B” fail to learn inverse relations “B is A”. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> demonstrates that small-scale LLMs can learn and generalize compositional skills through fine-tuning on tasks involving skill combinations. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> provide mechanistic analyses on how LLMs tackle compositional reasoning tasks. Our work conducts experiments on the tasks from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> and finds that LLMs fail on composite tasks when logical steps are intermixed. Our work further provides empirical and theoretical analysis on why the composition can succeed or fail and introduces an improved method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Experimental Details and More Results</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Details of the dataset and setup</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the dataset from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, and construct 9 composite tasks for our experiments. The details can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, while here we provide some illustrations for convenience.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The composite tasks are compositions using eight simple tasks listed in <a href="#A2.T4" title="In B.1 Details of the dataset and setup ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details and More Results ‣ Can Language Models Compose Skills In-Context?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
We use these simple tasks to construct the following composite tasks: opposition+swap (named ’oppopair swap’ in the code), opposition+pastTense (’oppoverb’), pastTense+swap (’verbpair swap’), capitalization+swap (’upperswap’), swap+capitalization (’swapupper’), capitalization+twoSum (’upper twosum’), pastTense+plusOne (’verbsingle plusone’), pastTense+capitalization (’verbsingle upper’), plusOne+capitalization (’plusone upper’).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
